# Configuring a Bundler: Webpack

Running Webpack with no configuration at all is fine for a test. Real projects want control: a different entry file, output somewhere else, a build tuned for debugging rather than for shipping. All of that lives in one file.

Webpack looks for **`webpack.config.js`** in your project directory. If it exists, Webpack reads it; if not, you get the defaults from the previous reading.

## The Shape of the File

```javascript
// webpack.config.js
module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.js',
  output: {
    filename: 'main.js'
  },
  mode: 'development'
};
```

That `module.exports` is the CommonJS export syntax. Webpack loads this file with Node, and Node's default module system is CommonJS, which is why configuration files are written this way rather than with `export default`.

Webpack has a very large number of options, organized into categories in its documentation. Three of them do most of the work.

## `entry`

The file Webpack starts from, and therefore the root of the dependency graph:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.js'
};
```

`./src/index.js` is the default. If your main file is called something else, say the entry to match:

```javascript
  entry: './src/my_code.js'
```

## `output`

Where the bundle is written. It takes two properties worth knowing.

**`filename`** is the name of the bundle, `main.js` by default:

```javascript
  output: {
    filename: 'out.js'
  }
```

Worth knowing: changing this does not clean up after the old one. Build to `main.js`, then rename the output to `out.js`, and `dist/` now contains both files, one of them stale.

**`path`** is the directory to write into, `dist` by default. It has one requirement that catches everybody: **it must be an absolute path.** A relative `'output'` will not do.

You could paste in an absolute path, and you should not:

```javascript
  output: {
    path: '/home/alice/code/my-webpack-project/output'
  }
```

That works on exactly one computer. Anyone else who checks out the project, and you on your other machine, get a path that does not exist.

Instead, build the absolute path at run time. Node gives every file a variable **`__dirname`**, the directory that file is in, so this is portable:

```javascript
  output: {
    path: __dirname + '/output'
  }
```

And the form you will see most often in real configuration files:

```javascript
const path = require('path');

module.exports = {
  output: {
    filename: 'out.js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'output')
  }
};
```

`path` is a module built into Node, and `path.resolve` takes pieces of a path and returns one absolute path from them. It handles the separators for you, which is the difference between the two forms: your string concatenation assumes `/`, and `path.resolve` is right on every operating system.

Note that this runtime provides a simplified `path` module with `join`, `basename`, `dirname`, and `extname` on it, and no `resolve`, so treat the configurations on this page as files to write rather than cells to run.

## `mode`

This one changes the character of the build.

```javascript
  mode: 'development'
```

**`development`** optimizes for build speed and for debugging. The output is longer, spread over many lines, and keeps comments explaining Webpack's own machinery. It is readable when something has gone wrong.

**`production`** optimizes the result. The bundle comes out minified, effectively on one line, with almost nothing left that a human would want to read. It is the smallest, fastest thing to download.

Use `development` while you are building and `production` for what you deploy. The two are not interchangeable: shipping a development build means sending every visitor a much larger file, and debugging a production build means reading minified code.

## Putting It Together

```javascript
// webpack.config.js
const path = require('path');

module.exports = {
  entry: './src/my_code.js',
  output: {
    filename: 'out.js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'output')
  },
  mode: 'development'
};
```

```
$ npx webpack
```

No arguments needed. Webpack finds the configuration file itself.

## Exercises

1. Write a configuration that starts from `./src/app.js`, writes `bundle.js` into a `build` directory next to the configuration file, and produces a build optimized for deployment. Use `__dirname` for the path rather than `path.resolve`, so the cell can run here.

    ```{node}
    // webpack.config.js

    module.exports = {
      // Write your configuration here
    };

    console.log(module.exports);
    ```
    ```+solution
    // webpack.config.js

    module.exports = {
      entry: './src/app.js',
      output: {
        filename: 'bundle.js',
        path: __dirname + '/build'
      },
      mode: 'production'
    };

    console.log(module.exports);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(module.exports.entry).should('equal', './src/app.js').run('Set `entry` to `./src/app.js`', 'Set the entry point');
    wrap(module.exports.output.filename).should('equal', 'bundle.js').run('Set `output.filename` to `bundle.js`', 'Named the output file');
    wrap(typeof module.exports.output.path === 'string' && module.exports.output.path.endsWith('/build')).should('equal', true).run('Set `output.path` to `__dirname + \'/build\'`, so the directory is next to the configuration file on any machine', 'Set the output directory');
    wrap(module.exports.mode).should('equal', 'production').run('A build for deployment should use `production` mode, which minifies the bundle', 'Chose the deployment mode');
    ```

2. Why write `path.resolve(__dirname, 'output')` instead of an absolute path like `/home/alice/project/dist`?

    ```{mcq}
    What does building the path at run time buy you?
    correct: Correct! `__dirname` is wherever the file happens to be, so the path is right on any machine and any operating system, and `path.resolve` gets the separators right too.
    wrong: Think about what happens to a hardcoded absolute path when a colleague clones the project onto a different computer.
    [x] It works on any computer, wherever the project directory happens to be
    [ ] It makes the bundle run faster in the browser
    [ ] It hides your folder names for security
    [ ] It makes Webpack delete the old output before building
    ```

3. You are in the middle of building a feature and want readable code and clear errors in the browser. Which mode, and why?

    ```{mcq}
    Which mode fits development work?
    correct: Correct! `development` skips minification and keeps the output readable, which is what you want when you are trying to understand what went wrong.
    wrong: One mode optimizes the file for downloading, the other optimizes it for being read by a person.
    [x] `development`, because it skips minification and keeps the code readable
    [ ] `production`, because it makes the file as small as possible
    [ ] `production`, because it is the standard mode for all JavaScript
    [ ] `development`, because it uploads your code to the internet automatically
    ```

